{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Intersection of Sets.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Intersection of Sets.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"intersection of sets intersection of sets","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Intersection of Sets.md","fileName":"Intersection of Sets.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Intersection of Sets","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 00:29\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Set Relations and Algebraic Foundations]] · [[Set Theory and Relations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Set Relations and Algebraic Foundations","Set Theory and Relations","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Intersection of Sets.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Intersection of Sets.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"intersection of sets intersection of sets","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Intersection of Sets.md","fileName":"Intersection of Sets.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Intersection of Sets","heading":"Intersection of Sets","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The intersection of sets contains exactly the elements common to all of them. Intersections preserve many closure properties, so intersecting subgroups, subrings, or subfields again produces the corresponding substructure.\n\nThis makes intersection a natural way to define the smallest structure satisfying stated containment requirements.\n\nFor two sets, A ∩ B = {x | x ∈ A ∧ x ∈ B}; its definition mirrors logical conjunction. Sets are disjoint exactly when their intersection is empty.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Set Relations and Algebraic Foundations","Set Theory and Relations","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{